@@ -1,530 +0,0 @@
-"""
-Space-time cluster detection for the Burning Activity Calendar.
-
-Context
--------
-Today the anomaly detector sums every grid cell in the AOI into a single
-daily number (calendar_daily.csv) and looks for anomalies along that one
-time axis. That hides anything genuinely local: a real spatiotemporal
-spike confined to one corner of the AOI can be averaged away by normal
-activity everywhere else, and conversely a modest AOI-wide anomaly can
-really be one small area burning unusually intensely rather than a
-region-wide event. The review's fix is a proper space-time scan
-statistic instead of a purely temporal one.
-
-This module implements the retrospective space-time permutation scan
-statistic (Kulldorff 1997; Kulldorff, Heffernan, Hartman, Assuncao &
-Mostashari, 2005, "A space-time permutation scan statistic for disease
-outbreak detection", PLoS Medicine), adapted from disease counts to fire
-detections:
-
-* A candidate cluster is a cylinder: a spatial zone (a seed cell plus its
-  nearest neighbours on the SAME 0.5-degree grid calendar_cells.csv
-  already uses -- see docs/METHODOLOGY.md section 4 for why that grid
-  size -- so this does not introduce a second, uncoordinated spatial
-  resolution) crossed with a contiguous run of days.
-* The "permutation" null model needs no population-at-risk denominator
-  (which this project does not have): under the null of no space-time
-  interaction, a cylinder's expected count is
-      expected = (total detections in the zone, all days)
-               * (total detections in the window, all cells)
-               / (grand total detections in the whole study region)
-  i.e. location and date are independent. This is exactly Kulldorff's
-  permutation model, and the reason it is a good fit here: it only
-  needs the detections this project already has, not a population
-  baseline.
-* Kulldorff's (1997) log-likelihood-ratio statistic ranks every
-  candidate cylinder; the most likely cluster is the one with the
-  largest LLR. Significance is a Monte Carlo p-value: the date of every
-  detection is randomly permuted (independently of its cell) many
-  times, which preserves BOTH marginals exactly -- each cell's total
-  count over the whole period, and each day's total count over the
-  whole AOI -- and the same scan is re-run on each permuted copy to
-  build a null distribution of the maximum LLR (Dwass 1957 / Kulldorff
-  1997). A cluster's p-value is the share of the null replicates whose
-  own best cylinder is at least as extreme.
-
-Composes with the other review items already shipped:
-* Run this on ``pyroscope.pipeline.fire_type.vegetation_fires_only(df)``,
-  not the raw hotspot table, so a flare field (steady, type 2) is never
-  scanned as if it were a wildfire cluster.
-* The clusters returned here are a DIFFERENT kind of evidence from a
-  ``pyroscope.stats.climatology.changepoint_regimes`` regime: a regime
-  is a change in the WHOLE AOI's mean over a (typically long) stretch;
-  a cylinder here is a LOCAL excess over a (typically short) one. They
-  will often, but need not, agree, and neither replaces the other.
-* If you want an FDR-controlled flag across the clusters this module
-  returns (rather than reading ``p_value`` directly against a fixed
-  alpha), feed ``[c.p_value for c in clusters]`` into
-  ``pyroscope.stats.fdr.benjamini_hochberg``.
-
-Performance. For ``n_cells`` grid cells, a spatial-zone cap of
-``max_spatial_cells``, a temporal-window cap of ``max_window_days`` and
-``n_days`` days in the study period, one full scan costs
-O(n_cells * max_spatial_cells * max_window_days * n_days) -- every
-window length is evaluated for every nested zone with one vectorized
-numpy pass over all start days, which is why that is tractable at all.
-Monte Carlo significance repeats the full scan ``n_sims`` times. The
-defaults below deliberately scan for SUB-SEASONAL, LOCAL clusters (this
-project's own anomaly_events.csv episodes run 3-10 days; AOI-wide,
-multi-year regime shifts are what changepoint_regimes is for instead),
-which keeps the window cap small; even so, this is the most expensive
-diagnostic in the pipeline and is meant to be run deliberately on a
-calendar already narrowed to an AOI, not on a continental or global run
-(see review item 8). Measured on a grid the size of this project's own
-shipped Egypt example (101 cells at the default 0.5-degree grid, 4,000+
-days of history) with the default spatial/temporal caps: one full scan
-is about 3-4 seconds, so the default PYRO_SCAN_N_SIMS=199 is roughly
-12-15 minutes end to end (minimum achievable p-value 1/200 = 0.005).
-Lower PYRO_SCAN_N_SIMS for quick iteration; raise it (Kulldorff's own
-papers typically use 999) for a final run, and lower
-PYRO_SCAN_MAX_SPATIAL_CELLS / PYRO_SCAN_MAX_WINDOW_DAYS if that is still
-too slow for your AOI.
-
-No dependency beyond numpy; pandas is optional and only used by the two
-``*_from_detections`` / ``*_from_cell_day_frame`` convenience wrappers
-that build the cell x day table for you.
-"""
